          "quote_en": "MSA is an architecture style that defines and creates systems through the use of small independent and self-contained services that align closely with business activities. An individual microservice is a service that is implemented with a single purpose, that is self-contained, and that is independent of other instances and services. Microservices are the primary Architecture Building Blocks (ABBs) of an MSA. As part of an Enterprise Architecture, MSA brings a layer of service capabilities to extend the business capabilities of the enterprise and, where the enterprise is a small self-contained service, MSA may be perceived to be the Enterprise Architecture. Refer to The Open Group White Paper: Microservices Architecture [W169].",
          "source_path": "togaf-standard-10th/G21Ie.pdf",
          "source_sha256": "279f6c06124f49a147a168591965805115b2fc976eea2664e41e491ac5d163bf"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "G21I:1.1:rule02",
      "kind": "source-semantic-rule",
      "statement_zh": "服务粒度、业务域对齐与清晰接口用于限制变更影响并理解依赖。",
      "boundary_zh": "独立性不意味着没有依赖；应记录实际消费者、接口契约与运行约束。",
      "evidence": [
